Phased Refactoring Strategy

To reduce platform dependencies and accelerate the initial production deployment, the refactoring approach is structured into two implementation phases.

This approach allows the engineering team to deliver the core functionality required for the upcoming release while progressively introducing the full platform architecture in subsequent releases.


Phase 1 – Productionisation of Core Reauthoring Capability

Phase 1 focuses on transitioning the PoC solution into a production-ready service capable of supporting AI-assisted document reauthoring and validation workflows.

To simplify the initial production deployment, this phase introduces a streamlined architecture that consolidates the reauthoring and validation pipelines into a single service and removes several platform components that are not required for the first release.

The Phase 1 architecture includes:

• CTB Workbench UI for document upload and review
• A FastAPI-based service implementing the reauthoring and validation workflows
• Document preprocessing and chunking pipelines
• Integration with the enterprise AI platform through the UDI SDK
• Storage of processed documents and outputs within the authorised GCP storage platform

Several advanced platform capabilities that were explored during the PoC are intentionally deferred to a later phase. These include workflow orchestration frameworks, knowledge graph generation, vector embeddings, and governed service gateways.

This simplified architecture allows the team to focus on stabilising the core functionality while ensuring that the platform is deployed within the authorised cloud environment.


Phase 2 – Knowledge Platform and Workflow Architecture

Phase 2 will extend the platform architecture to introduce the full knowledge management capabilities envisioned for the CTB programme.

This phase will introduce additional platform components required to support scalable AI workflows and enterprise knowledge management use cases.

The Phase 2 architecture will introduce:

• Event-driven workflow orchestration using LangGraph
• Decoupled AI processing workflows for reauthoring, validation, knowledge extraction, and embeddings
• Ontology-driven knowledge graph generation
• Vector embedding generation and semantic retrieval services
• Governed access to storage and platform services through the MCP gateway
• Hybrid knowledge retrieval APIs to support chatbot and workbench query capabilities

These capabilities will enable the platform to evolve from a document processing system into a scalable knowledge platform supporting policy and procedure intelligence across the organisation.


Proposed Repository Structure

To support the transition from the current PoC implementation to the production platform architecture, the CTB codebase will be restructured into a set of modular repositories aligned with the phased architecture approach.

The repository structure for Phase 1 focuses on stabilising the application services required for document processing and user interaction.

The Phase 2 repository structure will introduce additional platform repositories required to support workflow orchestration, knowledge processing, and platform governance capabilities.


Refactored Code Flow Overview

The refactored architecture introduces a structured processing pipeline that separates document ingestion, AI processing workflows, and user interaction into independent platform components.

In Phase 1, document processing begins when a document is uploaded through the CTB Workbench UI.

The document is processed by the ingestion pipeline, which performs preprocessing and structural extraction. The document is converted into a normalized format and divided into logical sections that can be processed independently.

The reauthoring workflow applies a set of LLM-powered agents to rewrite document sections into a simplified and standardized format. The validation workflow then evaluates the generated content against the original document to detect potential information loss or inconsistencies.

Following validation, the outputs are presented to users through the CTB Workbench UI for review and approval.

Future phases will introduce event-driven orchestration, knowledge extraction workflows, and vector embedding generation triggered after document approval.


Conclusion

The proposed refactoring approach provides a structured path for transitioning the current PoC implementation into a production-ready architecture aligned with the Codify the Bank (CTB) platform strategy.

By adopting a phased delivery approach, the platform can achieve early production deployment of the core document reauthoring capability while progressively introducing the broader knowledge platform architecture in subsequent releases.

This strategy allows the engineering team to stabilise the core application services while ensuring that the platform remains aligned with the long-term architectural vision.

The engineering refactoring backlog outlined in this document provides a prioritized set of implementation activities required to achieve this transition.




1. Structural Normalisation (Canonicalisation)

Converting unstructured or semi-structured documents into a consistent canonical representation (e.g., structured markdown or section-based format) where document structure cannot be reliably derived using deterministic parsing alone.

⸻

2. Semantic Segmentation / Chunking

Identifying logical content boundaries based on meaning rather than fixed rules, particularly for complex or inconsistently structured documents.

⸻

3. Content Classification and Tagging

Assigning document types, categories, or business metadata based on contextual understanding of content.

⸻

4. Content Transformation and Standardisation

Applying semantic transformations such as summarisation, rewriting, simplification, or normalisation into a consistent target format or style.

⸻

5. Entity and Relationship Extraction

Extracting structured artefacts (e.g., entities, obligations, controls, relationships) from unstructured text, often aligned to an ontology or business model.

⸻

6. Validation and Semantic Comparison

Comparing source and generated content to detect omissions, inconsistencies, or gaps based on meaning rather than exact text matching.

⸻

7. Embedding Generation for Semantic Retrieval

Generating vector representations of document content using embedding models to enable semantic search, similarity matching, and retrieval use cases.
This capability is distinct from generative LLM usage and is optimised for vectorisation rather than text generation.

